/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436022 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f049c9a7-cc49-5c3f-a4df-0714b2f9933a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436023 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "11ea0465-98a8-532d-bb2d-293a976cf74c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436024 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "30fcae88-a1e2-5970-b696-299270bb46fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "adf8aa6a-75ee-5eec-b556-6b1e3d610f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436026 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(-2,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "563f43ed-8e03-5890-8ba0-fe3a95b9c80d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436026 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,-1) & =4{(-2)}^{2}+9{(-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4(4)+9(1) \\\\  & =16+9 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the $25$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=25.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436027 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(3,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{x-y}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af624627-f936-5c7e-a5ee-772c5d7da48f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436027 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =\\frac{2}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4 & =x-y \\\\ y & =x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the line $y=x-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={y}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5ccf563-8a57-5f1f-ad06-ef72dc726ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [0,\\infty ),$ the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
